WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.287
We introduce Dex-One2Many, a framework that learns generalizable dexterous manipulation from a single human video.

2
00:00:07.687 --> 00:00:17.452
Existing methods use the human video as execution-level guidance: they track the hand and object motion in the video and train the robot to reproduce that exact trajectory.

3
00:00:17.452 --> 00:00:24.072
Trained in simulation and deployed zero-shot on the real robot, such a policy works in the configuration shown in the video.

4
00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:26.673
What if the configuration changes?

5
00:00:26.673 --> 00:00:32.673
With the object placed differently, the demonstrated trajectory no longer fits, and the policy fails.

6
00:00:32.673 --> 00:00:38.217
To handle configurations beyond the ones shown in the video, we propose Dex-One2Many.

7
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:47.217
Trained from the same single human video, Dex-One2Many completes the task in configurations the video never showed.

8
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:53.837
Our key idea is to abstract the video into stage-wise scene graphs, and let RL learn how to act.

9
00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:58.708
The input is a single human video of the task.

10
00:00:58.908 --> 00:01:11.694
A vision-language model abstracts the video into one scene graph per stage. Each edge is a predicate, a hand–object or object–object relation such as grasp or inside; the stage sequence gives their order.

11
00:01:11.894 --> 00:01:16.108
Object poses, goal poses, and grasps are left free.

12
00:01:16.238 --> 00:01:22.747
Each stage graph serves as a generative constraint for sampling reset states; here, Stage 3.

13
00:01:22.847 --> 00:01:28.133
First, the anchor object, the pot, is placed anywhere in the workspace.

14
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:33.519
Then Pikachu is sampled where the pre-inside predicate holds, near the pot.

15
00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:42.079
For the grasp predicate, the hand is placed at one of many synthesized multi-finger grasps, each with a verified closing command.

16
00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:48.893
Each sampled state is instantiated in simulation and kept only if it is collision-free and stable.

17
00:01:48.993 --> 00:01:58.160
Repeating this yields many reset states from one graph: different pot positions, object poses, and grasps that all satisfy the same relations.

18
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:08.417
The same procedure is applied to every stage, from the empty-hand start to the goal, reproducing the video's graph sequence in simulation.

19
00:02:08.517 --> 00:02:13.089
Ten thousand verified states per stage form the initial-state distribution.

20
00:02:14.729 --> 00:02:20.729
The graph is embodiment-agnostic: only the robot model and its grasp set are swapped.

21
00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:26.486
Four arm–hand embodiments share one task specification.

22
00:02:27.116 --> 00:02:34.544
RL episodes start uniformly from the reset states of every stage, in tens of thousands of parallel environments.

23
00:02:34.574 --> 00:02:44.524
For visualization, the environments are arranged in rows by the stage their episode starts from, Stage 4 at the top and Stage 0 at the bottom.

24
00:02:44.554 --> 00:02:48.439
Training from stage-wise resets proceeds as follows.

25
00:02:48.469 --> 00:02:53.755
With dense predicate rewards, the stages nearest the goal are learned first.

26
00:02:53.785 --> 00:02:58.714
Success then propagates backward, since the later stages are already solved.

27
00:02:58.754 --> 00:03:04.039
Eventually the full task is solved from the empty-hand start, stage 0.

28
00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:10.896
In ablations, removing stage-wise resets drops success from over 90 % to about 16 %.

29
00:03:10.936 --> 00:03:16.222
The same procedure trains all four embodiments; each learns the full task.

30
00:03:16.252 --> 00:03:20.823
The trained policy completes the task from an empty hand.

31
00:03:23.023 --> 00:03:29.738
We evaluate on 500 episodes per embodiment, with initial and goal positions sampled across the workspace.

32
00:03:29.878 --> 00:03:37.663
No grasp is retargeted from the video; the policy adopts different grasp types depending on where the object is.

33
00:03:37.713 --> 00:03:45.183
The other embodiments are evaluated the same way; across five tasks, the four average about 95 % success.

34
00:03:45.283 --> 00:03:51.998
Each embodiment learns its own grasps for the same task structure, from the same single video.

35
00:03:52.037 --> 00:04:00.029
In simulation, each task is learned from one human video for four arm–hand embodiments — only the robot model and its grasp set change.

36
00:04:00.029 --> 00:04:07.029
Direct manipulation: grasp the object and move it to the target configuration.

37
00:04:07.029 --> 00:04:14.029
Success rates over 500 episodes per task, with initial object positions sampled across the workspace.

38
00:04:14.029 --> 00:04:21.029
Tool use: grasp a tool and act on another object — the same predicate rewards, no task-specific design.

39
00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:28.176
Across five tasks, the four embodiments average 94.6–96.8 % success.

40
00:04:28.176 --> 00:04:39.483
Zero-shot sim-to-real on UR3 + Wuji 1. On configurations never shown in the video: 60–85 % success, where the baselines stay at or below 15 %.

